1
00:00:05,980 --> 00:00:07,040
Let's get started

2
00:00:07,040 --> 00:00:09,200
Today we're going to be talking about exploration

3
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:12,400
We're going to be starting by talking about exploration

4
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:15,960
in standard reinforcement learning settings

5
00:00:15,960 --> 00:00:18,680
actually, primarily, in bandit settings

6
00:00:18,680 --> 00:00:20,640
and then how that might translate to robotics

7
00:00:20,640 --> 00:00:21,850
and large language models

8
00:00:21,850 --> 00:00:23,520
And then we're actually going to revisit

9
00:00:23,520 --> 00:00:25,730
some of the meta-learning topics that we talked about

10
00:00:25,730 --> 00:00:29,800
on Wednesday and revisit how we might effectively learn how

11
00:00:29,800 --> 00:00:33,800
to explore in meta-RL settings

12
00:00:33,800 --> 00:00:36,600
So what is the exploration problem

13
00:00:36,600 --> 00:00:41,000
If we apply reinforcement learning to some problems

14
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:42,500
we're able to solve them quite well

15
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:46,400
So for example, if we try to train a reinforcement learning

16
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:48,560
agent to play Breakout, where the reward is

17
00:00:48,560 --> 00:00:53,920
the score of the game, you get a pretty performant player that's

18
00:00:53,920 --> 00:00:57,800
able to get a high score

19
00:00:57,800 --> 00:01:02,130
But if you instead try to train a reinforcement learning agent

20
00:01:02,130 --> 00:01:04,730
to play a game like this one on the right, which

21
00:01:04,730 --> 00:01:08,370
is called Montezuma's Revenge, it's

22
00:01:08,370 --> 00:01:10,970
really, really hard for reinforcement learning systems

23
00:01:10,970 --> 00:01:13,450
to play

24
00:01:13,450 --> 00:01:15,890
Now, why is there this difference

25
00:01:15,890 --> 00:01:21,970
So in the second game, you can get a reward if you get a key

26
00:01:21,970 --> 00:01:23,850
Kind of intuitively, if you're getting a key

27
00:01:23,850 --> 00:01:27,090
that's useful for being able to progress

28
00:01:27,090 --> 00:01:29,730
Opening a door is also useful

29
00:01:29,730 --> 00:01:33,350
And there's this skull that's rolling around

30
00:01:33,350 --> 00:01:36,490
And intuitively, we as humans know

31
00:01:36,490 --> 00:01:38,970
that that's probably something you might want to avoid

32
00:01:38,970 --> 00:01:42,850
Likewise, falling off of a ledge might be something

33
00:01:42,850 --> 00:01:43,670
that you'd avoid

34
00:01:43,670 --> 00:01:47,050
But these are things that the agent doesn't know itself

35
00:01:47,050 --> 00:01:51,210
And in general, kind of completing the game

36
00:01:51,210 --> 00:01:53,890
these are very sparse, discrete events

37
00:01:53,890 --> 00:01:56,850
And completing the game only weakly correlates

38
00:01:56,850 --> 00:01:59,860
relates with those rewarding events

39
00:01:59,860 --> 00:02:02,500
And so actually, each of those individual rewarding events

40
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:04,860
are also kind of few and far between

41
00:02:04,860 --> 00:02:06,380
So for example, at the beginning

42
00:02:06,380 --> 00:02:09,860
the agent isn't getting any reward at all

43
00:02:09,860 --> 00:02:12,500
and it has to figure out how to even get to the key

44
00:02:12,500 --> 00:02:14,380
in the first place, which requires quite

45
00:02:14,380 --> 00:02:17,740
a long sequence of events

46
00:02:17,740 --> 00:02:20,180
Now, as humans, we kind of know what

47
00:02:20,180 --> 00:02:22,500
to do because we understand what each

48
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:26,020
of these sprites in the game mean

49
00:02:26,020 --> 00:02:28,420
But the algorithm really doesn't know that

50
00:02:28,420 --> 00:02:30,700
So if we instead try to imagine ourselves

51
00:02:30,700 --> 00:02:33,140
as reinforcement learning algorithms

52
00:02:33,140 --> 00:02:34,760
let's think through that a little bit

53
00:02:34,760 --> 00:02:39,180
And we can think through that with a game called Mao

54
00:02:39,180 --> 00:02:44,570
It's like half a game, half a joke in some ways

55
00:02:44,570 --> 00:02:47,980
You aren't told the rules of the game in advance

56
00:02:47,980 --> 00:02:52,090
And if you break a rule, then you get a penalty

57
00:02:52,090 --> 00:02:53,260
And you don't actually know

58
00:02:53,260 --> 00:02:56,730
You get a penalty, but you don't actually know what the rule is

59
00:02:56,730 --> 00:02:59,790
and you have to discover the rules through trial and error

60
00:02:59,790 --> 00:03:02,230
And when you get a penalty, it's not always clear

61
00:03:02,230 --> 00:03:03,910
why you got a penalty

62
00:03:03,910 --> 00:03:09,030
The rules don't actually necessarily make sense to you

63
00:03:09,030 --> 00:03:12,750
And tasks like Montezuma's Revenge

64
00:03:12,750 --> 00:03:17,170
are difficult because reinforcement learning methods

65
00:03:17,170 --> 00:03:18,690
they don't know about the rules

66
00:03:18,690 --> 00:03:20,910
They know very little about what's good

67
00:03:20,910 --> 00:03:24,070
and what's bad, what they can do, what they can't do

68
00:03:24,070 --> 00:03:28,190
And when tasks become longer and longer

69
00:03:28,190 --> 00:03:31,210
this also makes the problem even more difficult

70
00:03:31,210 --> 00:03:33,950
because you have to figure out an even longer sequence

71
00:03:33,950 --> 00:03:36,710
of actions that might lead to getting reward

72
00:03:36,710 --> 00:03:39,550
versus getting a penalty

73
00:03:39,550 --> 00:03:42,670
And so if you think about what the reinforcement learning

74
00:03:42,670 --> 00:03:45,430
agent has to do, reinforcement learning agents

75
00:03:45,430 --> 00:03:48,750
are kind of analogous to imagining if your goal in life

76
00:03:48,750 --> 00:03:54,120
was to win 50 games of Mao, and you also didn't even

77
00:03:54,120 --> 00:03:55,560
know this in advance

78
00:03:55,560 --> 00:03:59,220
You only kind of learned this from actually getting rewards

79
00:03:59,220 --> 00:04:04,120
from winning games and getting penalties from losing games

80
00:04:04,120 --> 00:04:08,000
So that's what the reinforcement learning system--

81
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:11,820
that's the difficulty that these algorithms are faced with

82
00:04:11,820 --> 00:04:13,400
It's really, really hard, especially

83
00:04:13,400 --> 00:04:16,839
in sparse-reward scenarios

84
00:04:16,839 --> 00:04:21,360
Now, if we were to think about this exploration problem

85
00:04:21,360 --> 00:04:24,600
there's a couple different ways of looking at it

86
00:04:24,600 --> 00:04:28,440
One way of looking at it is, we want to discover high reward

87
00:04:28,440 --> 00:04:30,120
strategies

88
00:04:30,120 --> 00:04:36,600
We want to basically seek out rewards and find sequences

89
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:39,080
of actions that, individually, those actions may not

90
00:04:39,080 --> 00:04:41,300
be rewarding, but when we seek them together

91
00:04:41,300 --> 00:04:44,840
we're able to find rewards

92
00:04:44,840 --> 00:04:49,440
And another way to view the exploration problem

93
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:52,740
is deciding whether to attempt a new behavior

94
00:04:52,740 --> 00:04:57,490
or continuing to do the best thing that it knows so far

95
00:04:57,490 --> 00:04:59,740
You're basically deciding, should I try something new

96
00:04:59,740 --> 00:05:04,700
or should I do something that has been good in the past

97
00:05:04,700 --> 00:05:07,780
These are actually part of a single problem

98
00:05:07,780 --> 00:05:10,740
where you need to--

99
00:05:10,740 --> 00:05:12,320
there's this exploitation problem

100
00:05:12,320 --> 00:05:15,320
which is doing what you know will yield the highest reward

101
00:05:15,320 --> 00:05:18,540
doing the things that you've seen before that have

102
00:05:18,540 --> 00:05:20,600
high reward, acting according to your Q function

103
00:05:20,600 --> 00:05:24,080
for example, and this exploration problem

104
00:05:24,080 --> 00:05:27,020
which is doing things that you haven't done before in hopes

105
00:05:27,020 --> 00:05:30,130
of getting higher reward

106
00:05:30,130 --> 00:05:32,420
And so that's how we can think about this reinforcement

107
00:05:32,420 --> 00:05:34,590
learning problem, where we need to balance these two

108
00:05:34,590 --> 00:05:37,300
objectives if our goal is to maximize

109
00:05:37,300 --> 00:05:42,540
a sum of rewards over time

110
00:05:42,540 --> 00:05:47,320
As some examples, if you are in a new city

111
00:05:47,320 --> 00:05:50,430
you move somewhere new, exploration

112
00:05:50,430 --> 00:05:52,140
would be trying new restaurants

113
00:05:52,140 --> 00:05:54,430
Exploitation would be going to your favorite restaurant

114
00:05:54,430 --> 00:05:58,230
or restaurant that you like the best

115
00:05:58,230 --> 00:06:00,830
One actually very common use of reinforcement learning

116
00:06:00,830 --> 00:06:05,950
is figuring out which ads to place and where

117
00:06:05,950 --> 00:06:09,070
And exploitation would be taking the ad that

118
00:06:09,070 --> 00:06:13,830
has been the most successful and showing that one to the user

119
00:06:13,830 --> 00:06:15,590
An exploration would be instead, trying

120
00:06:15,590 --> 00:06:18,030
to pick a different advertisement at random

121
00:06:18,030 --> 00:06:24,550
and instead showing that advertisement to the user

122
00:06:24,550 --> 00:06:27,550
Or as a kind of a final example, in oil drilling

123
00:06:27,550 --> 00:06:30,640
you don't know where you're going to find--

124
00:06:30,640 --> 00:06:31,930
where you're going to find oil

125
00:06:31,930 --> 00:06:34,350
And so exploitation would be taking the best location

126
00:06:34,350 --> 00:06:36,990
that you've found before that has yielded oil

127
00:06:36,990 --> 00:06:44,050
versus exploration would be drilling at a new location

128
00:06:44,050 --> 00:06:46,910
So exploration is quite hard

129
00:06:46,910 --> 00:06:51,420
And if we want to design an algorithm for exploration

130
00:06:51,420 --> 00:06:55,000
we might think about what is the best strategy for exploring

131
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:57,120
and the best way to trade off between exploration

132
00:06:57,120 --> 00:06:59,440
and exploitation

133
00:06:59,440 --> 00:07:01,880
And we also need to think about, what

134
00:07:01,880 --> 00:07:05,800
does optimality of exploration even mean

135
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:09,720
There's a range of reinforcement learning problems

136
00:07:09,720 --> 00:07:11,760
ranging from more simple problems

137
00:07:11,760 --> 00:07:14,920
where you can actually analyze whether or not

138
00:07:14,920 --> 00:07:19,320
the strategy is optimal or not, to more complicated problems

139
00:07:19,320 --> 00:07:23,940
where it's intractable to analyze whether or it's optimal

140
00:07:23,940 --> 00:07:26,280
and also intractable to develop algorithms

141
00:07:26,280 --> 00:07:30,620
that are able to do a good job at exploration

142
00:07:30,620 --> 00:07:35,200
On the simple side, you start with multi-armed bandits

143
00:07:35,200 --> 00:07:39,120
the kind of one-step RL problems that don't have any state

144
00:07:39,120 --> 00:07:41,620
And then if you want to make that more complicated

145
00:07:41,620 --> 00:07:42,660
you add context

146
00:07:42,660 --> 00:07:46,850
You add some sort of features for each POS for the state

147
00:07:46,850 --> 00:07:47,710
that you're in

148
00:07:47,710 --> 00:07:51,850
You could then move to small MDPs, to larger MDPs

149
00:07:51,850 --> 00:07:54,900
And a lot of the kind of problems

150
00:07:54,900 --> 00:07:56,730
that we've been talking about in this course

151
00:07:56,730 --> 00:07:59,150
are large MDPs without any known structure

152
00:07:59,150 --> 00:08:03,200
and those are the ones that are like the hardest for getting

153
00:08:03,200 --> 00:08:05,330
good exploration strategy, and also, theoretically

154
00:08:05,330 --> 00:08:09,290
thinking about exploration

155
00:08:09,290 --> 00:08:13,290
So before we talk about exploration

156
00:08:13,290 --> 00:08:15,330
in the large problems, let's talk a little bit

157
00:08:15,330 --> 00:08:18,650
about bandits for a minute, because they provide a nice way

158
00:08:18,650 --> 00:08:19,990
to think about exploration

159
00:08:19,990 --> 00:08:23,010
And they're actually used in practice

160
00:08:23,010 --> 00:08:25,410
for a variety of problems

161
00:08:25,410 --> 00:08:29,450
And specifically, the way that we can think about--

162
00:08:29,450 --> 00:08:30,890
what is a bandit

163
00:08:30,890 --> 00:08:35,330
It's not the kind of someone that runs around and maybe tries

164
00:08:35,330 --> 00:08:36,929
to steal stuff or something

165
00:08:36,929 --> 00:08:43,980
It's a formulation of reinforcement learning

166
00:08:43,980 --> 00:08:52,500
where the horizon is just one time step for a single episode

167
00:08:52,500 --> 00:08:55,740
There is no state

168
00:08:55,740 --> 00:09:00,820
And unlike everything we've talked about in MDP so far

169
00:09:00,820 --> 00:09:07,140
your reward is actually stochastic

170
00:09:07,140 --> 00:09:11,210
And so if you go back to the examples before

171
00:09:11,210 --> 00:09:12,580
you could think about the problem

172
00:09:12,580 --> 00:09:16,420
as selecting a restaurant as a bandit problem where

173
00:09:16,420 --> 00:09:23,280
you could, for example, select to go to one restaurant

174
00:09:23,280 --> 00:09:25,720
You could select to go to a different restaurant

175
00:09:25,720 --> 00:09:28,620
You could select to go to a different restaurant

176
00:09:28,620 --> 00:09:30,380
and so forth

177
00:09:30,380 --> 00:09:32,820
And depending on your experience in previous episodes

178
00:09:32,820 --> 00:09:36,400
of selecting restaurants, you could inform your choice

179
00:09:36,400 --> 00:09:38,980
about whether you want to pick a new restaurant where you know

180
00:09:38,980 --> 00:09:42,630
nothing about the reward, or go to a restaurant

181
00:09:42,630 --> 00:09:47,470
that you've received high reward for it in the future

182
00:09:47,470 --> 00:09:49,670
And the reward is stochastic in the sense

183
00:09:49,670 --> 00:09:52,710
that your experience at that restaurant

184
00:09:52,710 --> 00:09:55,750
may not be the same every single time

185
00:09:55,750 --> 00:10:01,470
And so yeah, this is what a bandit problem looks like

186
00:10:01,470 --> 00:10:06,510
Now, if we want to think about how good is exploration

187
00:10:06,510 --> 00:10:08,390
and how good is our reinforcement

188
00:10:08,390 --> 00:10:11,750
learning algorithm, we want to think

189
00:10:11,750 --> 00:10:18,510
about how much reward we're getting relative to if we

190
00:10:18,510 --> 00:10:20,950
were acting optimally

191
00:10:20,950 --> 00:10:27,310
And so far, we've been thinking about sums of rewards

192
00:10:27,310 --> 00:10:32,230
oftentime averaged over episodes

193
00:10:32,230 --> 00:10:34,220
In bandit problems, you actually are

194
00:10:34,220 --> 00:10:36,550
often thinking about, actually, the entire reinforcement

195
00:10:36,550 --> 00:10:39,110
learning process, and the optimality of the reinforcement

196
00:10:39,110 --> 00:10:41,480
learning process, and the optimality of exploration

197
00:10:41,480 --> 00:10:43,280
followed by exploitation

198
00:10:43,280 --> 00:10:53,120
And so say that you have taken capital T episodes so far

199
00:10:53,120 --> 00:10:56,200
Then the best you could have done

200
00:10:56,200 --> 00:11:03,840
is going to be something like the expected reward of taking

201
00:11:03,840 --> 00:11:10,680
the optimal action times T. And this is actually in some ways

202
00:11:10,680 --> 00:11:15,000
an oracle, because when you haven't taken any episodes

203
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:18,440
at the beginning, then you are forced

204
00:11:18,440 --> 00:11:21,280
to take a random action because you don't know anything

205
00:11:21,280 --> 00:11:23,200
about the MDP so far

206
00:11:23,200 --> 00:11:24,980
And so this is kind of, in retrospect

207
00:11:24,980 --> 00:11:27,360
what is the best thing you could have done

208
00:11:27,360 --> 00:11:32,360
And this is a upper bound on the sum of rewards

209
00:11:32,360 --> 00:11:36,440
that you could be getting after T episodes

210
00:11:36,440 --> 00:11:40,110
And then if we want to think about how well we've been doing

211
00:11:40,110 --> 00:11:43,450
we can think about this subtracted by the sum of rewards

212
00:11:43,450 --> 00:11:46,770
that we actually observed from the actions that we've taken

213
00:11:46,770 --> 00:11:50,250
And so that would be something like a sum from T

214
00:11:50,250 --> 00:11:56,210
equals 1 to T of the action-- the actual action

215
00:11:56,210 --> 00:12:02,410
that we decided to take in each particular episode

216
00:12:02,410 --> 00:12:07,580
And the gap between these two is going to be measuring

217
00:12:07,580 --> 00:12:09,930
basically, a form of optimality gap

218
00:12:09,930 --> 00:12:14,850
And this expression is something that's called regret

219
00:12:14,850 --> 00:12:18,210
And it's measuring, basically, how much

220
00:12:18,210 --> 00:12:20,810
do we regret taking the action that we did compared

221
00:12:20,810 --> 00:12:30,210
to taking the optimal action

222
00:12:30,210 --> 00:12:34,730
Now, if you were to think about regret over time

223
00:12:34,730 --> 00:12:37,340
so here, actually, time is indexing over episodes

224
00:12:37,340 --> 00:12:40,940
because each episode is just a single time step

225
00:12:40,940 --> 00:12:45,380
So if we think about as a function

226
00:12:45,380 --> 00:12:51,580
of the number of episodes, the regret

227
00:12:51,580 --> 00:12:55,780
after taking those number of episodes

228
00:12:55,780 --> 00:13:00,700
If you were acting optimally, what sort of curve

229
00:13:00,700 --> 00:13:05,020
would you get on this plot

230
00:13:05,020 --> 00:13:09,680
Yeah, so I see mostly going straight

231
00:13:09,680 --> 00:13:12,500
So if you're acting optimally, this will be zero

232
00:13:12,500 --> 00:13:15,880
and it will be zero even as you have more and more episodes

233
00:13:15,880 --> 00:13:17,460
It'll be zero in expectation

234
00:13:17,460 --> 00:13:20,340
There might be some randomness

235
00:13:20,340 --> 00:13:26,340
And so you would get a curve that looks like this

236
00:13:26,340 --> 00:13:31,580
And so this is sort of optimal

237
00:13:31,580 --> 00:13:33,740
Now, in practice, actually achieving

238
00:13:33,740 --> 00:13:37,500
this is not really possible because at the first time step

239
00:13:37,500 --> 00:13:43,390
like I mentioned, you have to take a random action

240
00:13:43,390 --> 00:13:46,510
Now, does anyone have thoughts on what this curve would

241
00:13:46,510 --> 00:13:51,510
look like if you were just taking an episode at random

242
00:13:51,510 --> 00:13:52,730
Yeah, it'll keep increasing

243
00:13:52,730 --> 00:13:53,430
Yeah

244
00:13:53,430 --> 00:13:57,700
So if you're taking an episode at random

245
00:13:57,700 --> 00:13:59,280
there will now be a gap between these

246
00:13:59,280 --> 00:14:00,950
and you would be getting a higher reward

247
00:14:00,950 --> 00:14:03,710
from taking the optimal action compared to taking

248
00:14:03,710 --> 00:14:05,510
that action at random

249
00:14:05,510 --> 00:14:12,230
And if, for example, you were always picking A1

250
00:14:12,230 --> 00:14:16,710
and A1 is not the best action, then after one episode

251
00:14:16,710 --> 00:14:24,670
you would expect to have some kind of non-zero regret

252
00:14:24,670 --> 00:14:26,850
So you would have a curve that looks like this

253
00:14:26,850 --> 00:14:30,590
And then if you took A1 again the next episode

254
00:14:30,590 --> 00:14:37,970
then you would then incur the same difference

255
00:14:37,970 --> 00:14:42,630
in optimal rewards versus the rewards that you took

256
00:14:42,630 --> 00:14:46,170
And so then this would increase, and you would actually

257
00:14:46,170 --> 00:14:48,970
get something that looks linear

258
00:14:48,970 --> 00:14:52,410
And that means that, basically, every time you--

259
00:14:52,410 --> 00:14:54,090
basically, at every time step, there's

260
00:14:54,090 --> 00:14:58,170
a fixed difference between the optimal reward and the reward

261
00:14:58,170 --> 00:14:59,490
that you took

262
00:14:59,490 --> 00:15:05,150
And so whenever you get a linear regret increasing linearly

263
00:15:05,150 --> 00:15:09,090
that means that your algorithm is not actually improving

264
00:15:09,090 --> 00:15:11,050
It's not actually using the exploration

265
00:15:11,050 --> 00:15:15,130
to get better rewards

266
00:15:15,130 --> 00:15:19,570
If instead you were--

267
00:15:19,570 --> 00:15:22,510
the blue markers never worked that well, but we can see

268
00:15:22,510 --> 00:15:25,090
If instead you were actually having an algorithm that

269
00:15:25,090 --> 00:15:29,670
is a bit smarter, then you would expect to have sublinear regret

270
00:15:29,670 --> 00:15:32,860
And so you'd expect actually not just linearly increase

271
00:15:32,860 --> 00:15:34,380
your regret over time, but actually

272
00:15:34,380 --> 00:15:37,060
have this curve look something like this

273
00:15:37,060 --> 00:15:41,560
where it starts looking something more like linear

274
00:15:41,560 --> 00:15:45,220
And then eventually, if you then get to the optimal behavior

275
00:15:45,220 --> 00:15:48,300
after exploring, then it flattens out

276
00:15:48,300 --> 00:15:49,600
to something like this

277
00:15:49,600 --> 00:15:55,220
And you could imagine that this portion is like you're now

278
00:15:55,220 --> 00:15:57,420
in the exploitation phase

279
00:15:57,420 --> 00:15:59,180
And the phase before that is when

280
00:15:59,180 --> 00:16:04,900
you're exploring and gathering information about the problem

281
00:16:04,900 --> 00:16:07,280
The question is, to make these curves

282
00:16:07,280 --> 00:16:09,780
are we running the learning process a lot of different times

283
00:16:09,780 --> 00:16:12,430
and then averaging

284
00:16:12,430 --> 00:16:14,060
So for these curves, this is actually--

285
00:16:14,060 --> 00:16:18,220
each of these curves is just one reinforcement learning process

286
00:16:18,220 --> 00:16:21,800
And I guess they're very smooth here

287
00:16:21,800 --> 00:16:26,140
and in practice, they'll be a little bit noisier

288
00:16:26,140 --> 00:16:29,850
The optimal, I guess, this is kind of an expectation

289
00:16:29,850 --> 00:16:32,610
And so this is going to be smooth

290
00:16:32,610 --> 00:16:36,990
But you might have something where, for example, in reality

291
00:16:36,990 --> 00:16:40,310
maybe you got unlucky one time step

292
00:16:40,310 --> 00:16:44,570
And this went up because you got unlucky

293
00:16:44,570 --> 00:16:47,150
Maybe at the next time step, you still took the optimal action

294
00:16:47,150 --> 00:16:49,390
but got lucky

295
00:16:49,390 --> 00:16:53,550
And so this will actually have some noise in it in practice

296
00:16:53,550 --> 00:16:55,550
Yeah, so the question is, we have an expectation

297
00:16:55,550 --> 00:16:56,250
on the first part

298
00:16:56,250 --> 00:16:57,670
We don't have an expectation here

299
00:16:57,670 --> 00:17:00,260
Why is that

300
00:17:00,260 --> 00:17:03,550
This is basically some measure of the optimal expected

301
00:17:03,550 --> 00:17:06,950
reward after T time steps

302
00:17:06,950 --> 00:17:10,050
This is actually just the actual reward that you observed

303
00:17:10,050 --> 00:17:13,079
You could also have an expectation over this

304
00:17:13,079 --> 00:17:15,190
over reinforcement learning runs

305
00:17:15,190 --> 00:17:19,950
And that would then give you--

306
00:17:19,950 --> 00:17:22,930
as an example here, if you run this algorithm multiple times

307
00:17:22,930 --> 00:17:24,310
these regret curves will actually

308
00:17:24,310 --> 00:17:25,849
look a little bit different depending

309
00:17:25,849 --> 00:17:26,829
on how unlucky you get

310
00:17:26,829 --> 00:17:29,000
And then if you were to then measure

311
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:30,920
this in terms of expectations, then that

312
00:17:30,920 --> 00:17:33,200
would be averaging over different runs

313
00:17:33,200 --> 00:17:35,720
of the reinforcement learning algorithm

314
00:17:35,720 --> 00:17:39,360
Yeah, so the question is, if we're taking random actions

315
00:17:39,360 --> 00:17:43,240
why is it linear, and why is it increasing by a fixed amount

316
00:17:43,240 --> 00:17:54,570
So this will be noisy if you are taking random actions

317
00:17:54,570 --> 00:17:58,400
I don't know how to say that

318
00:17:58,400 --> 00:17:59,820
So yeah, it will be noisy

319
00:17:59,820 --> 00:18:03,720
If you run it multiple times in expectation, it will be linear

320
00:18:03,720 --> 00:18:06,720
And if you have a deterministic MDP

321
00:18:06,720 --> 00:18:09,880
and you always select action 1 through a very not

322
00:18:09,880 --> 00:18:14,000
smart strategy, and the rewards are a bit more deterministic

323
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:17,000
then you might actually get a curve that looks like this

324
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:19,820
So the question is, if we're always taking A1

325
00:18:19,820 --> 00:18:24,000
then how do we actually know how to measure this formula

326
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,510
This is not actually a formula that--

327
00:18:26,510 --> 00:18:28,730
sorry

328
00:18:28,730 --> 00:18:30,690
To back up a little bit, this is actually not--

329
00:18:30,690 --> 00:18:34,040
this is what we're aiming to-- we're aiming to minimize regret

330
00:18:34,040 --> 00:18:35,410
And we're aiming to get something

331
00:18:35,410 --> 00:18:37,130
that is able to flatten out

332
00:18:37,130 --> 00:18:40,490
But this is more of an object that's used for analysis

333
00:18:40,490 --> 00:18:42,470
and not our optimization objective

334
00:18:42,470 --> 00:18:46,210
because we actually can't measure this

335
00:18:46,210 --> 00:18:49,570
without knowledge of the bandit problem

336
00:18:49,570 --> 00:18:51,650
or knowledge of the MDP

337
00:18:51,650 --> 00:18:53,590
If you introduce an expectation here

338
00:18:53,590 --> 00:18:55,970
then you would expect it to be monotonic

339
00:18:55,970 --> 00:19:00,050
If not, then because the reward is stochastic

340
00:19:00,050 --> 00:19:03,450
you could have scenarios where you

341
00:19:03,450 --> 00:19:12,210
say that the best action is A5, and the probability

342
00:19:12,210 --> 00:19:14,970
of getting good reward is--

343
00:19:14,970 --> 00:19:18,090
I don't know-- 90% of the time, and 10% of the time

344
00:19:18,090 --> 00:19:19,330
you get a bad reward

345
00:19:19,330 --> 00:19:24,120
Then you're going to see, when you get a good reward

346
00:19:24,120 --> 00:19:29,780
it will actually relative to the expert which is averaging over

347
00:19:29,780 --> 00:19:31,440
that, it will go down

348
00:19:31,440 --> 00:19:33,730
But if you write this as an expectation

349
00:19:33,730 --> 00:19:35,440
for the reinforcement learning algorithm

350
00:19:35,440 --> 00:19:37,660
it would be monotonic

351
00:19:37,660 --> 00:19:38,620
Cool

352
00:19:38,620 --> 00:19:42,340
So this is the regret formula, regret

353
00:19:42,340 --> 00:19:45,340
after doing reinforcement learning for T episodes

354
00:19:45,340 --> 00:19:47,320
Expected reward of the best action

355
00:19:47,320 --> 00:19:49,620
basically, the best we can hope for in expectation

356
00:19:49,620 --> 00:19:55,350
if we had knowledge of what the bandit is

357
00:19:55,350 --> 00:19:57,060
And then this is the reward of the action

358
00:19:57,060 --> 00:20:00,380
that's actually taken

359
00:20:00,380 --> 00:20:03,700
There's a variety of simple strategies for exploration

360
00:20:03,700 --> 00:20:09,300
and including some that you can show that are optimal

361
00:20:09,300 --> 00:20:11,180
And you can often analyze algorithms

362
00:20:11,180 --> 00:20:18,100
and provide theoretical guarantees on regret

363
00:20:18,100 --> 00:20:19,900
And then also, even the algorithms

364
00:20:19,900 --> 00:20:23,030
that you can theoretically make some guarantees

365
00:20:23,030 --> 00:20:27,750
about in practice, performance can vary

366
00:20:27,750 --> 00:20:30,950
And so to get a little bit more intuition-- actually, no

367
00:20:30,950 --> 00:20:33,410
Before we do that-- we're going to play a game in a minute

368
00:20:33,410 --> 00:20:34,790
but before we do that, let's talk

369
00:20:34,790 --> 00:20:37,670
about different strategies for exploration

370
00:20:37,670 --> 00:20:44,430
so specifically in bandits, so not in MDPs

371
00:20:44,430 --> 00:20:47,450
So one approach is when you're selecting different restaurants

372
00:20:47,450 --> 00:20:52,070
for example, if you haven't explored a restaurant

373
00:20:52,070 --> 00:20:54,630
then you could choose to be optimistic

374
00:20:54,630 --> 00:20:56,270
about that restaurant

375
00:20:56,270 --> 00:20:59,550
And that will incentivize you to explore new restaurants

376
00:20:59,550 --> 00:21:02,390
rather than always being greedy with respect to restaurants

377
00:21:02,390 --> 00:21:04,630
you've tried before

378
00:21:04,630 --> 00:21:06,150
And so the way this could work is

379
00:21:06,150 --> 00:21:07,870
you could track the average reward

380
00:21:07,870 --> 00:21:13,230
that you have received in the past for each of the actions

381
00:21:13,230 --> 00:21:15,710
And then if you were to exploit, you

382
00:21:15,710 --> 00:21:18,590
would just take the action that has

383
00:21:18,590 --> 00:21:22,000
the maximum average reward for the things

384
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:23,880
that you've seen so far

385
00:21:23,880 --> 00:21:27,200
But instead, if we want to actually encourage the algorithm

386
00:21:27,200 --> 00:21:30,400
to visit new things, then we could--

387
00:21:30,400 --> 00:21:32,560
we still want to visit actions that we

388
00:21:32,560 --> 00:21:34,480
think have high average rewards, so that if we

389
00:21:34,480 --> 00:21:36,100
go to a restaurant that's really bad

390
00:21:36,100 --> 00:21:37,880
we maybe don't go to it again

391
00:21:37,880 --> 00:21:42,120
But we're going to add a term that is some kind of variance

392
00:21:42,120 --> 00:21:44,560
of our reward estimate

393
00:21:44,560 --> 00:21:47,300
This is basically like, if this variance is high

394
00:21:47,300 --> 00:21:49,640
that means that we're uncertain about the reward

395
00:21:49,640 --> 00:21:52,080
for that action

396
00:21:52,080 --> 00:21:53,840
And if we're uncertain, then we're

397
00:21:53,840 --> 00:21:56,100
going to basically give this sort of bonus

398
00:21:56,100 --> 00:21:58,280
this sort of reward bonus, on top

399
00:21:58,280 --> 00:22:00,380
of our estimate of the average reward

400
00:22:00,380 --> 00:22:03,360
to encourage our algorithm to explore

401
00:22:03,360 --> 00:22:07,380
things that are uncertain, or things that have high variance

402
00:22:07,380 --> 00:22:09,000
And so the intuition here is that we're

403
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:12,400
assuming that unknown things are going to be good until we

404
00:22:12,400 --> 00:22:14,380
actually then observe them

405
00:22:14,380 --> 00:22:16,740
And if we then observe them and they're really bad

406
00:22:16,740 --> 00:22:19,380
then we'll act accordingly

407
00:22:19,380 --> 00:22:21,090
In the future, the variance will go down

408
00:22:21,090 --> 00:22:22,890
and the reward estimate will go down

409
00:22:22,890 --> 00:22:28,070
And this is a way to balance exploration and exploitation

410
00:22:28,070 --> 00:22:30,530
Yeah, so the question is, how would you choose in this case

411
00:22:30,530 --> 00:22:32,410
an action that you haven't seen before

412
00:22:32,410 --> 00:22:34,610
So if you haven't seen an action before

413
00:22:34,610 --> 00:22:38,250
then your variance estimate will be something like infinite

414
00:22:38,250 --> 00:22:39,650
for example

415
00:22:39,650 --> 00:22:45,730
And then this will encourage it to explore, basically

416
00:22:45,730 --> 00:22:47,930
all of the actions that it hasn't explored first

417
00:22:47,930 --> 00:22:49,570
at least once

418
00:22:49,570 --> 00:22:51,290
And then from there, your variance

419
00:22:51,290 --> 00:22:54,290
will go-- your variance estimate or your uncertainty

420
00:22:54,290 --> 00:22:56,970
about an action will go down over time

421
00:22:56,970 --> 00:23:00,330
And yeah, so this is basically, if you haven't done anything

422
00:23:00,330 --> 00:23:05,130
before, it will incentivize it to visit that first

423
00:23:05,130 --> 00:23:05,850
Cool

424
00:23:05,850 --> 00:23:08,030
So this is a popular algorithm

425
00:23:08,030 --> 00:23:10,720
You can actually derive guarantees for it

426
00:23:10,720 --> 00:23:13,050
It's called upper confidence bound, or one version of it

427
00:23:13,050 --> 00:23:14,820
is called upper confidence bound

428
00:23:14,820 --> 00:23:16,190
There's another approach that you

429
00:23:16,190 --> 00:23:21,530
can also take that is actually harder to show guarantees for

430
00:23:21,530 --> 00:23:24,030
but can work pretty well in practice

431
00:23:24,030 --> 00:23:26,640
And we actually saw something like this in the last lecture

432
00:23:26,640 --> 00:23:28,350
And essentially, what you're trying to do

433
00:23:28,350 --> 00:23:31,470
is you're trying to estimate the probability of getting

434
00:23:31,470 --> 00:23:36,830
good rewards from each of the arms of the bandit

435
00:23:36,830 --> 00:23:39,070
And so the way this would work is

436
00:23:39,070 --> 00:23:44,070
you assume that there's some sort of distribution of rewards

437
00:23:44,070 --> 00:23:47,070
for each of the actions

438
00:23:47,070 --> 00:23:52,470
And this defines a partially observed MDP

439
00:23:52,470 --> 00:23:55,070
where you don't know the parameters of the reward

440
00:23:55,070 --> 00:23:55,570
function

441
00:23:55,570 --> 00:23:57,510
You don't know if you're going to get

442
00:23:57,510 --> 00:24:00,030
a good reward or bad reward

443
00:24:00,030 --> 00:24:02,390
And so you'll then actually try to model and form

444
00:24:02,390 --> 00:24:05,830
an estimate of the likelihood of getting rewards

445
00:24:05,830 --> 00:24:08,230
by trying to model these parameters in your uncertainty

446
00:24:08,230 --> 00:24:10,480
over it

447
00:24:10,480 --> 00:24:12,150
So you'll form an estimate with any data

448
00:24:12,150 --> 00:24:14,110
that you've seen so far

449
00:24:14,110 --> 00:24:18,920
Then you'll actually sample an MDP

450
00:24:18,920 --> 00:24:22,960
from your model of possible MDPs of the world

451
00:24:22,960 --> 00:24:25,420
and then you'll take the optimal action

452
00:24:25,420 --> 00:24:28,960
assuming that your sample was correct

453
00:24:28,960 --> 00:24:32,520
and then repeat that process to update your beliefs again

454
00:24:32,520 --> 00:24:38,080
and then sample a new MDP, and repeat the process

455
00:24:38,080 --> 00:24:39,980
And we'll walk through an example

456
00:24:39,980 --> 00:24:42,520
But the intuition here is that you can iteratively

457
00:24:42,520 --> 00:24:47,200
improve your model of the bandit and act according

458
00:24:47,200 --> 00:24:50,360
to one of the models that is covered

459
00:24:50,360 --> 00:24:55,280
by one of the potential sets of probabilities that's

460
00:24:55,280 --> 00:24:58,880
modeled by your MDP model

461
00:24:58,880 --> 00:25:00,720
One last note, these are actually

462
00:25:00,720 --> 00:25:03,880
methods that are widely used and studied

463
00:25:03,880 --> 00:25:08,170
in industry for recommenders and ad placement

464
00:25:08,170 --> 00:25:09,920
Everything we've been talking about so far

465
00:25:09,920 --> 00:25:13,070
is for these sorts of small bandit problems

466
00:25:13,070 --> 00:25:16,130
but they are actually quite relevant for a number

467
00:25:16,130 --> 00:25:20,930
of practical problems, even when you don't have longer episodes

468
00:25:20,930 --> 00:25:23,970
or episodes with state

469
00:25:23,970 --> 00:25:26,090
And now, let's play a little game

470
00:25:26,090 --> 00:25:28,270
to get some intuition for how this works

471
00:25:28,270 --> 00:25:35,090
So here is a little simulation

472
00:25:35,090 --> 00:25:38,410
Say that you are hired as the head of drug development

473
00:25:38,410 --> 00:25:41,050
at a prestigious research hospital

474
00:25:41,050 --> 00:25:48,110
Things are going well, and now there's some sort of plague

475
00:25:48,110 --> 00:25:49,650
and you need to figure out what drugs

476
00:25:49,650 --> 00:25:52,590
are going to be effective for addressing the plague

477
00:25:52,590 --> 00:25:56,250
And you want to save people's lives by giving them

478
00:25:56,250 --> 00:25:59,770
the drug that's most effective

479
00:25:59,770 --> 00:26:05,390
Now, you can recognize this as a bandit problem, where each drug

480
00:26:05,390 --> 00:26:08,730
you don't know how capable it's going to be

481
00:26:08,730 --> 00:26:11,400
And we can say that maybe there is--

482
00:26:11,400 --> 00:26:13,660
maybe there's somewhere around 5,000 patients

483
00:26:13,660 --> 00:26:15,980
and there's maybe around 10 different drugs that

484
00:26:15,980 --> 00:26:17,780
you're considering

485
00:26:17,780 --> 00:26:21,820
And your goal is to try to understand-- well, try

486
00:26:21,820 --> 00:26:26,860
to save lives, try to save as many lives as possible

487
00:26:26,860 --> 00:26:32,320
but also balancing saving lives right now versus saving lives

488
00:26:32,320 --> 00:26:33,900
in the future as you learn information

489
00:26:33,900 --> 00:26:36,980
about each of the drugs

490
00:26:36,980 --> 00:26:38,340
Cool

