Patient died

545
00:28:00,550 --> 00:28:01,670
Yeah

546
00:28:01,670 --> 00:28:02,790
Eight

547
00:28:02,790 --> 00:28:05,260
Patient died

548
00:28:05,260 --> 00:28:06,270
Seven

549
00:28:06,270 --> 00:28:07,340
Patient lived

550
00:28:07,340 --> 00:28:09,840
All right, should we just keep on going seven at this point

551
00:28:09,840 --> 00:28:11,960
Are we happy with seven

552
00:28:11,960 --> 00:28:13,600
Try nine

553
00:28:13,600 --> 00:28:16,160
Patient lived

554
00:28:16,160 --> 00:28:17,000
Try one

555
00:28:17,000 --> 00:28:18,400
Try one

556
00:28:18,400 --> 00:28:20,720
Patient died

557
00:28:20,720 --> 00:28:23,480
Four, patient lived

558
00:28:23,480 --> 00:28:24,840
Four again

559
00:28:24,840 --> 00:28:29,080
Patient died

560
00:28:29,080 --> 00:28:32,360
Nine, patient lived

561
00:28:32,360 --> 00:28:35,480
Three, patient died

562
00:28:35,480 --> 00:28:37,870
Nine, patient lived

563
00:28:37,870 --> 00:28:40,540
OK, we like nine

564
00:28:40,540 --> 00:28:41,040
Cool

565
00:28:41,040 --> 00:28:44,840
Maybe let's keep on doing nine

566
00:28:44,840 --> 00:28:46,140
Patient is living a lot

567
00:28:46,140 --> 00:28:48,620
Great

568
00:28:48,620 --> 00:28:50,440
We have one patient died

569
00:28:50,440 --> 00:28:55,640
So let's say that we kept on going with nine for a while

570
00:28:55,640 --> 00:28:57,320
And then we can plot--

571
00:28:57,320 --> 00:29:00,440
actually, well, with 5,000, which I could just say keep

572
00:29:00,440 --> 00:29:01,560
on doing nine

573
00:29:01,560 --> 00:29:04,300
Maybe we'll stop there and we can actually plot the regret

574
00:29:04,300 --> 00:29:10,030
So this was the true efficacy of the drugs

575
00:29:10,030 --> 00:29:12,150
and we can see that nine actually was the best one

576
00:29:12,150 --> 00:29:15,650
so we actually found the best one, which is good

577
00:29:15,650 --> 00:29:17,630
Seven was also pretty good

578
00:29:17,630 --> 00:29:19,210
And then we could also plot the number

579
00:29:19,210 --> 00:29:21,910
of lives that are saved by different algorithms

580
00:29:21,910 --> 00:29:23,930
And so higher here is better

581
00:29:23,930 --> 00:29:26,000
And we see that the posterior sampling approach--

582
00:29:26,000 --> 00:29:28,330
And in this case, what posterior sampling would be doing

583
00:29:28,330 --> 00:29:31,690
is modeling the probability of the efficacy of each drug

584
00:29:31,690 --> 00:29:33,670
And then based on this estimate of that

585
00:29:33,670 --> 00:29:37,010
picking something that's better

586
00:29:37,010 --> 00:29:39,510
And we can see that how well we did right here is in purple

587
00:29:39,510 --> 00:29:41,510
It's a little bit hard to tell just because it's

588
00:29:41,510 --> 00:29:43,610
a little small, but we can see that, generally, it

589
00:29:43,610 --> 00:29:48,930
looks like we're doing pretty well relative to the algorithms

590
00:29:48,930 --> 00:29:51,470
And then you can also plot regret over time

591
00:29:51,470 --> 00:29:54,050
And actually, here we can see that we're actually doing better

592
00:29:54,050 --> 00:29:54,910
than all the algorithms

593
00:29:54,910 --> 00:29:56,080
So you guys are pretty good

594
00:29:56,080 --> 00:29:57,610
Nice

595
00:29:57,610 --> 00:29:59,230
Maybe there's some luck involved there

596
00:29:59,230 --> 00:30:00,850
so this is just a single run

597
00:30:00,850 --> 00:30:03,220
And what these different algorithms are is you see greedy

598
00:30:03,220 --> 00:30:04,300
is showing green

599
00:30:04,300 --> 00:30:07,020
Greedy is just picking the best thing you've seen so far

600
00:30:07,020 --> 00:30:09,550
And over time, especially with more and more trials

601
00:30:09,550 --> 00:30:11,420
that you might be running, like more and more

602
00:30:11,420 --> 00:30:13,000
patients, more and more ad placements

603
00:30:13,000 --> 00:30:15,490
whatever, it's getting a lot worse

604
00:30:15,490 --> 00:30:17,700
In this case, we saw that posterior sampling, yellow

605
00:30:17,700 --> 00:30:20,660
is by far the best algorithm

606
00:30:20,660 --> 00:30:24,620
And then the other algorithms, red is showing epsilon greedy

607
00:30:24,620 --> 00:30:26,820
Blue is showing UCB, so that's the first algorithm

608
00:30:26,820 --> 00:30:28,580
we talked about with being optimistic

609
00:30:28,580 --> 00:30:30,380
about uncertain actions

610
00:30:30,380 --> 00:30:32,580
So this is basically showing the trade-off

611
00:30:32,580 --> 00:30:34,630
between different algorithms

612
00:30:34,630 --> 00:30:36,460
One other thing to note that epsilon greedy

613
00:30:36,460 --> 00:30:37,900
it's a bit more greedy, and so we

614
00:30:37,900 --> 00:30:39,780
see that with-- when you have fewer patients

615
00:30:39,780 --> 00:30:41,720
it actually is doing better than UCB

616
00:30:41,720 --> 00:30:43,780
But then in the long run, UCB is doing

617
00:30:43,780 --> 00:30:47,780
better because it's actually optimizing a bit more

618
00:30:47,780 --> 00:30:50,800
for making sure that you're exploring more actions

619
00:30:50,800 --> 00:30:52,860
And so in the long run, it's able to take more

620
00:30:52,860 --> 00:30:55,700
optimal actions in the future

621
00:30:55,700 --> 00:30:57,700
Is there a mathematical reason why

622
00:30:57,700 --> 00:31:04,190
posterior sampling does better than UCB and epsilon greedy

623
00:31:04,190 --> 00:31:04,690
Yeah

624
00:31:04,690 --> 00:31:07,910
So I don't want to go too much into the math

625
00:31:07,910 --> 00:31:11,030
And actually, posterior sampling, for this MDP

626
00:31:11,030 --> 00:31:15,710
you probably could analyze it

627
00:31:15,710 --> 00:31:18,630
But in general, it can be hard to derive

628
00:31:18,630 --> 00:31:21,310
general theoretical guarantees for it

629
00:31:21,310 --> 00:31:29,190
With regard to epsilon greedy, I guess some of it

630
00:31:29,190 --> 00:31:31,550
comes down to that value of epsilon

631
00:31:31,550 --> 00:31:34,030
and then tuning it to be--

632
00:31:34,030 --> 00:31:36,190
and generally, with epsilon greedy

633
00:31:36,190 --> 00:31:41,070
you often choose epsilon to be smaller and smaller over time

634
00:31:41,070 --> 00:31:44,870
And that hyperparameter would probably drastically impact

635
00:31:44,870 --> 00:31:47,750
performance, because at this point in training

636
00:31:47,750 --> 00:31:50,590
if epsilon is constant, you're going to be keeping on--

637
00:31:50,590 --> 00:31:52,150
you're going to be still occasionally

638
00:31:52,150 --> 00:31:53,630
taking random drugs

639
00:31:53,630 --> 00:31:56,010
And at that point, you already know a lot about the drug

640
00:31:56,010 --> 00:32:01,330
and prescribing a random drug is a very poor choice

641
00:32:01,330 --> 00:32:02,170
Cool

642
00:32:02,170 --> 00:32:05,450
So also, if you want to play more, you can--

643
00:32:05,450 --> 00:32:09,050
I'll put the website in the slides

644
00:32:09,050 --> 00:32:12,170
So that's exploration in bandits

645
00:32:12,170 --> 00:32:15,490
Now, what does this look like for robotics, language models

646
00:32:15,490 --> 00:32:17,690
and larger MDPs

647
00:32:17,690 --> 00:32:19,770
Different people have different opinions on this

648
00:32:19,770 --> 00:32:23,850
I have a somewhat pessimistic opinion on it

649
00:32:23,850 --> 00:32:25,490
And in particular, there's actually

650
00:32:25,490 --> 00:32:30,170
a lot of research that try to translate these ideas to larger

651
00:32:30,170 --> 00:32:31,410
MDPs

652
00:32:31,410 --> 00:32:34,330
For example, you can try to formulate

653
00:32:34,330 --> 00:32:38,210
some estimate of whether you've taken an action or not

654
00:32:38,210 --> 00:32:40,910
and give reward bonuses for novelty

655
00:32:40,910 --> 00:32:44,650
for visiting new states, for taking new actions

656
00:32:44,650 --> 00:32:47,230
And similarly, like posterior sampling

657
00:32:47,230 --> 00:32:51,210
you can try to figure out models of different dynamics

658
00:32:51,210 --> 00:32:54,130
and so forth and act accordingly

659
00:32:54,130 --> 00:32:57,380
But at the end of the day, when you're in a very large MDP

660
00:32:57,380 --> 00:33:01,060
exploration from scratch is generally intractable

661
00:33:01,060 --> 00:33:03,660
So for example, if you randomly initialize a transformer

662
00:33:03,660 --> 00:33:07,580
language model and try to figure out

663
00:33:07,580 --> 00:33:12,780
how to make a user happy, or some other reward function

664
00:33:12,780 --> 00:33:15,300
like upvotes or something like that

665
00:33:15,300 --> 00:33:18,140
actually, just using upvotes to train a language

666
00:33:18,140 --> 00:33:21,420
model from scratch isn't going to work

667
00:33:21,420 --> 00:33:26,100
The sheer vastness of the possible space of tokens

668
00:33:26,100 --> 00:33:30,260
is way too large, at least relative to the patients

669
00:33:30,260 --> 00:33:33,060
that most users will have

670
00:33:33,060 --> 00:33:34,900
Likewise, in robotics, if you're trying

671
00:33:34,900 --> 00:33:37,380
to even just learn how to--

672
00:33:37,380 --> 00:33:41,580
I don't know-- pour water from one cup to another cup

673
00:33:41,580 --> 00:33:44,300
you're going to be stuck in a realm where you're just randomly

674
00:33:44,300 --> 00:33:46,580
moving arms around, rather than actually

675
00:33:46,580 --> 00:33:49,260
doing things that are useful if you're starting completely

676
00:33:49,260 --> 00:33:52,540
from scratch

677
00:33:52,540 --> 00:33:56,110
Instead, generally, what exploration

678
00:33:56,110 --> 00:34:00,590
looks like in these larger domains is to rely heavily

679
00:34:00,590 --> 00:34:05,190
on demonstrations, or on base models that were pre-trained

680
00:34:05,190 --> 00:34:07,570
on data and demonstrations

681
00:34:07,570 --> 00:34:09,710
So for example, reinforcement learning

682
00:34:09,710 --> 00:34:11,989
has been quite successful in language models

683
00:34:11,989 --> 00:34:14,310
because, and particularly in scenarios

684
00:34:14,310 --> 00:34:16,469
where you have a strong base model that already

685
00:34:16,469 --> 00:34:18,389
knows how to write coherent sentences

686
00:34:18,389 --> 00:34:21,190
and already knows roughly how to approach

687
00:34:21,190 --> 00:34:24,389
certain kinds of prompts, or certain kinds of problems

688
00:34:24,389 --> 00:34:28,429
And likewise, in robotics, if you have demonstrations

689
00:34:28,429 --> 00:34:31,070
of example behavior that achieve high reward

690
00:34:31,070 --> 00:34:35,870
then using that as a starting point for reinforcement learning

691
00:34:35,870 --> 00:34:39,429
is very powerful

692
00:34:39,429 --> 00:34:42,550
And generally, these models are providing an initialization

693
00:34:42,550 --> 00:34:44,750
that's really excellent for exploration

694
00:34:44,750 --> 00:34:48,350
and seeking out potential things

695
00:34:48,350 --> 00:34:50,830
Wherever feasible, it's also quite useful

696
00:34:50,830 --> 00:34:52,870
to use shaped rewards, because that

697
00:34:52,870 --> 00:34:56,280
can guide any sort of exploration process, as well

698
00:34:56,280 --> 00:34:58,780
And this could be if you're in simulations

699
00:34:58,780 --> 00:35:01,860
You can hand code sparse, dense rewards

700
00:35:01,860 --> 00:35:04,760
or if you're learning single-step rewards

701
00:35:04,760 --> 00:35:07,120
from preferences, that actually also provides

702
00:35:07,120 --> 00:35:09,760
a fairly dense reward signal

703
00:35:09,760 --> 00:35:12,760
This isn't to say you could also combine some of these ideas

704
00:35:12,760 --> 00:35:15,760
with demonstrations and base models

705
00:35:15,760 --> 00:35:19,520
But generally, you get a lot further

706
00:35:19,520 --> 00:35:24,280
from starting with this kind of initialization

707
00:35:24,280 --> 00:35:27,780
That said, there is still potentially an exploration

708
00:35:27,780 --> 00:35:29,780
problem that we started to talk about last time

709
00:35:29,780 --> 00:35:32,800
And I want to revisit that, now that we've actually

710
00:35:32,800 --> 00:35:39,040
started to learn about exploration in standard MDPs

711
00:35:39,040 --> 00:35:39,940
Yeah, exactly

712
00:35:39,940 --> 00:35:44,700
So the idea here is that if you're in these kinds of MDPs

713
00:35:44,700 --> 00:35:48,560
the state space is enormous, and all of the possible actions you

714
00:35:48,560 --> 00:35:50,640
can take is enormous, as well

715
00:35:50,640 --> 00:35:54,290
And this is a way to bias the model

716
00:35:54,290 --> 00:35:56,010
towards a part of the state space

717
00:35:56,010 --> 00:36:01,040
that is going to get high reward

718
00:36:01,040 --> 00:36:03,290
Yeah, giving it bias of, these are the kinds of things

719
00:36:03,290 --> 00:36:05,840
I want you to do

720
00:36:05,840 --> 00:36:08,730
So in particular, what I'd like to talk about is

721
00:36:08,730 --> 00:36:12,010
say you have some model that has been trained

722
00:36:12,010 --> 00:36:13,470
and it can do a variety of things

723
00:36:13,470 --> 00:36:15,770
But there's scenarios where when you're actually

724
00:36:15,770 --> 00:36:18,770
deploying that policy, you actually

725
00:36:18,770 --> 00:36:20,830
even if you have a lot of prior experience

726
00:36:20,830 --> 00:36:23,890
you might need to explore at test time

727
00:36:23,890 --> 00:36:25,970
because you might not have all the information you

728
00:36:25,970 --> 00:36:28,370
need to get high reward

729
00:36:28,370 --> 00:36:31,170
And so we talked about a couple examples on Wednesday

730
00:36:31,170 --> 00:36:33,930
where, for example, you might need to find ingredients

731
00:36:33,930 --> 00:36:34,870
in a kitchen

732
00:36:34,870 --> 00:36:36,910
And to do that, you can't just start cooking

733
00:36:36,910 --> 00:36:38,890
You need to open drawers, open cabinets

734
00:36:38,890 --> 00:36:42,570
and so forth to try to seek out information

735
00:36:42,570 --> 00:36:46,450
And second, in language models for reasoning

736
00:36:46,450 --> 00:36:48,570
you might need to explore different strategies that

737
00:36:48,570 --> 00:36:52,780
might lead to a solution to a problem

738
00:36:52,780 --> 00:36:56,460
rather than simply trying to start writing tokens that you

739
00:36:56,460 --> 00:36:59,980
think might lead to the solution

740
00:36:59,980 --> 00:37:02,420
Now, we talked about this kind of meta-RL problem

741
00:37:02,420 --> 00:37:07,540
where we're optimizing a policy with history, or with memory

742
00:37:07,540 --> 00:37:10,020
And generally, memory should allow

743
00:37:10,020 --> 00:37:13,140
agents to be able to explore and exploit

744
00:37:13,140 --> 00:37:15,660
because they're able to try different actions

745
00:37:15,660 --> 00:37:17,550
and remember what they did, and then use

746
00:37:17,550 --> 00:37:19,220
that information about where ingredients

747
00:37:19,220 --> 00:37:21,740
are to then solve the problem

748
00:37:21,740 --> 00:37:24,020
But we talked about how if we optimize exploration

749
00:37:24,020 --> 00:37:27,100
and exploitation together with an end-to-end objective

750
00:37:27,100 --> 00:37:27,900
this is hard

751
00:37:27,900 --> 00:37:29,400
Because we have this chicken and egg

752
00:37:29,400 --> 00:37:33,060
problem, where if we haven't yet learned how to explore, then

753
00:37:33,060 --> 00:37:35,820
we can't actually get high rewards because we

754
00:37:35,820 --> 00:37:37,460
haven't found the ingredients

755
00:37:37,460 --> 00:37:40,000
And likewise, if we don't yet know how to solve the task

756
00:37:40,000 --> 00:37:45,260
then we get low reward for any sort of exploration behavior

757
00:37:45,260 --> 00:37:48,430
So there's this coupling problem

758
00:37:48,430 --> 00:37:51,550
And we also talked about some alternative exploration

759
00:37:51,550 --> 00:37:54,030
strategies that are easier to optimize

760
00:37:54,030 --> 00:37:58,890
but can lead to suboptimal performance

761
00:37:58,890 --> 00:38:02,510
And what I'd like to talk about is

762
00:38:02,510 --> 00:38:06,510
there's actually a method that can both get optimal exploration

763
00:38:06,510 --> 00:38:09,870
and exploitation trade-off, and also be efficient to train

764
00:38:09,870 --> 00:38:12,780
So it sort of gets the best of both worlds

765
00:38:12,780 --> 00:38:14,150
And the idea for this is actually

766
00:38:14,150 --> 00:38:17,950
going to build upon this last alternative exploration

767
00:38:17,950 --> 00:38:19,870
strategy

768
00:38:19,870 --> 00:38:23,670
Let's write this right here

769
00:38:23,670 --> 00:38:30,150
So the idea here is that you can try to--

770
00:38:30,150 --> 00:38:32,210
for different environments or different tasks

771
00:38:32,210 --> 00:38:36,710
you could try to fit a dynamics model and a reward model

772
00:38:36,710 --> 00:38:41,310
and then try to explore in ways that tell us

773
00:38:41,310 --> 00:38:45,190
more information about our model of the environment

774
00:38:45,190 --> 00:38:50,120
And this should incentivize the agent or the policy

775
00:38:50,120 --> 00:38:52,040
to actually seek out additional information

776
00:38:52,040 --> 00:38:54,120
to explore in a way that's decoupled

777
00:38:54,120 --> 00:39:01,360
from executing on the task

778
00:39:01,360 --> 00:39:05,880
And so if you do this and you have different MDPs that

779
00:39:05,880 --> 00:39:08,960
correspond to navigating to different circles

780
00:39:08,960 --> 00:39:12,720
and then you try to incentivize it to take trajectories that

781
00:39:12,720 --> 00:39:14,160
lead to a better model of reward

782
00:39:14,160 --> 00:39:16,200
and better model of dynamics, you actually

783
00:39:16,200 --> 00:39:17,840
get a trajectory that looks something

784
00:39:17,840 --> 00:39:19,880
like the purple trajectory, where it's explicitly

785
00:39:19,880 --> 00:39:21,260
trying to visit states that allow

786
00:39:21,260 --> 00:39:26,920
it to learn where the reward function is going to be

787
00:39:26,920 --> 00:39:30,500
Now, this can learn efficient exploration strategies

788
00:39:30,500 --> 00:39:32,640
but it has its downsides and it can be

789
00:39:32,640 --> 00:39:35,320
suboptimal in some scenarios

790
00:39:35,320 --> 00:39:36,910
For example, if there's distractors

791
00:39:36,910 --> 00:39:40,100
like if there's pictures on the wall, for example

792
00:39:40,100 --> 00:39:42,640
then it's going to be looking at all the pictures on the wall

793
00:39:42,640 --> 00:39:49,170
to learn a good model of those different environments

794
00:39:49,170 --> 00:39:52,810
So we can build upon this idea and try

795
00:39:52,810 --> 00:39:57,410
to learn how to explore without learning a model

796
00:39:57,410 --> 00:40:01,570
And in particular, instead of trying to learn a dynamics

797
00:40:01,570 --> 00:40:04,410
and reward model, we could instead

798
00:40:04,410 --> 00:40:09,530
try to be able to predict what the task is

799
00:40:09,530 --> 00:40:14,370
So we're going to label each task with a identifier mu

800
00:40:14,370 --> 00:40:17,690
This could be either like a one-hot like an index

801
00:40:17,690 --> 00:40:21,150
or it could be a language description

802
00:40:21,150 --> 00:40:26,570
or it could be some more continuous description

803
00:40:26,570 --> 00:40:30,410
of the task, like continuous vector

804
00:40:30,410 --> 00:40:33,650
And then what we could do is we could

805
00:40:33,650 --> 00:40:38,010
try to explore in a way that allows us to predict

806
00:40:38,010 --> 00:40:40,610
what the task is at hand

807
00:40:40,610 --> 00:40:46,780
So for example, we'll have our say

808
00:40:46,780 --> 00:40:48,900
that we have two policies, and exploration policy

809
00:40:48,900 --> 00:40:50,700
and exploitation policy

810
00:40:50,700 --> 00:40:53,060
In practice you could combine these

811
00:40:53,060 --> 00:40:59,000
but it's helpful to think about them as separate

812
00:40:59,000 --> 00:41:02,960
So we'll have our exploration strategy or exploration policy

813
00:41:02,960 --> 00:41:09,300
This is going to produce some experience in a particular task

814
00:41:09,300 --> 00:41:11,640
And our goal is for it to be able to explore

815
00:41:11,640 --> 00:41:13,780
to be able to discern different tasks

816
00:41:13,780 --> 00:41:17,340
And so we could separately train a model

817
00:41:17,340 --> 00:41:20,180
that's going to be predicting the task

818
00:41:20,180 --> 00:41:26,100
identifier from the experience that we've collected so far

819
00:41:26,100 --> 00:41:28,620
And if we do something like this

820
00:41:28,620 --> 00:41:32,060
then this sort of exploration objective

821
00:41:32,060 --> 00:41:36,300
is completely separate from actually executing on the task

822
00:41:36,300 --> 00:41:40,800
The goal here is to be able to predict the task identifier

823
00:41:40,800 --> 00:41:42,760
And then separate from this, we could

824
00:41:42,760 --> 00:41:48,960
train a policy that's trying to execute on the task

825
00:41:48,960 --> 00:41:53,480
just using the identifier for the task

826
00:41:53,480 --> 00:41:56,040
And this is just multi-task learning

827
00:41:56,040 --> 00:41:57,660
or multi-task reinforcement learning

828
00:41:57,660 --> 00:42:02,340
and this we can train just with optimizing reward

829
00:42:02,340 --> 00:42:06,200
And it doesn't rely on having good exploration

830
00:42:06,200 --> 00:42:09,820
So here, we're able to decouple the objective for execution

831
00:42:09,820 --> 00:42:11,400
so it doesn't rely on exploration

832
00:42:11,400 --> 00:42:14,600
and have an exploration objective just based

833
00:42:14,600 --> 00:42:19,320
on identifying the task that isn't dependent on how well you

834
00:42:19,320 --> 00:42:21,530
do the task

835
00:42:21,530 --> 00:42:24,160
So on the slides, we'll train a policy in a task identification

836
00:42:24,160 --> 00:42:28,360
model so that we collect data that allows for accurate task

837
00:42:28,360 --> 00:42:29,840
prediction

838
00:42:29,840 --> 00:42:31,320
And then we'll also train a policy

839
00:42:31,320 --> 00:42:35,600
that's conditioned on the task ID

840
00:42:35,600 --> 00:42:40,440
in order to maximize reward for each task

841
00:42:40,440 --> 00:42:42,150
So which part of this is the exploration

842
00:42:42,150 --> 00:42:44,650
Is it the model that predicts the identifier

843
00:42:44,650 --> 00:42:47,210
So the exploration is really this policy right here

844
00:42:47,210 --> 00:42:50,090
And this model is actually just going

845
00:42:50,090 --> 00:42:52,230
to be used for the reward for this

846
00:42:52,230 --> 00:42:54,650
And so we're going to be--

847
00:42:54,650 --> 00:42:57,290
the reward for this policy will be, basically

848
00:42:57,290 --> 00:43:01,610
how well this model is identifying the task

849
00:43:01,610 --> 00:43:07,490
or basically, the negative error of this task identifier model

850
00:43:07,490 --> 00:43:08,230
Good question

851
00:43:08,230 --> 00:43:10,050
The question is, does this generalize

852
00:43:10,050 --> 00:43:12,130
to new unseen task categories

853
00:43:12,130 --> 00:43:16,850
This can actually struggle with generalization

854
00:43:16,850 --> 00:43:18,470
and we'll talk about that

855
00:43:18,470 --> 00:43:21,050
Let's go through meta-testing, and then we'll talk about that

856
00:43:21,050 --> 00:43:24,260
So this is roughly how training works

857
00:43:24,260 --> 00:43:25,890
And then at test time if you've trained

858
00:43:25,890 --> 00:43:27,510
all of these different things, then you

859
00:43:27,510 --> 00:43:28,910
can take your exploration policy

860
00:43:28,910 --> 00:43:33,010
run it to collect experience, then feed that experience

861
00:43:33,010 --> 00:43:36,450
into your task identifier model to get a task ID

862
00:43:36,450 --> 00:43:39,380
and then take that task ID, and feed that as input

863
00:43:39,380 --> 00:43:43,120
to your execution policy to then execute the task

864
00:43:43,120 --> 00:43:46,390
So you'll then explore and then identify the task

865
00:43:46,390 --> 00:43:49,740
and then exploit

866
00:43:49,740 --> 00:43:53,980
Now, getting back to that question, the upside of this

867
00:43:53,980 --> 00:43:56,320
is you don't need to model dynamics or rewards

868
00:43:56,320 --> 00:43:59,020
You're just learning this model that's

869
00:43:59,020 --> 00:44:01,180
predicting the task identifier

870
00:44:01,180 --> 00:44:03,340
But if the task identifier is just

871
00:44:03,340 --> 00:44:06,580
a one-hot vector, for example, this probably

872
00:44:06,580 --> 00:44:10,060
isn't going to generalize to new tasks very well

873
00:44:10,060 --> 00:44:13,390
And we'll see a solution to that on the next slide

874
00:44:13,390 --> 00:44:15,220
The question is, what is the main difference

875
00:44:15,220 --> 00:44:19,420
from black box meta-RL that we talked about before

876
00:44:19,420 --> 00:44:23,100
So we are still sampling different MDPs

877
00:44:23,100 --> 00:44:25,700
The main difference is simply the objective

878
00:44:25,700 --> 00:44:29,140
for the exploration part

879
00:44:29,140 --> 00:44:31,760
So well, I guess there's two main differences

880
00:44:31,760 --> 00:44:34,160
One is the objective or the exploration part

881
00:44:34,160 --> 00:44:36,190
and then the second is that here we're

882
00:44:36,190 --> 00:44:39,630
going to be maximizing rewards, similar to black box meta-RL

883
00:44:39,630 --> 00:44:42,270
But we're actually going to be giving it this task identifier

884
00:44:42,270 --> 00:44:45,590
so that it doesn't need to rely on exploration

885
00:44:45,590 --> 00:44:47,470
or a good exploration strategy when trying

886
00:44:47,470 --> 00:44:50,110
to learn how to do the task

887
00:44:50,110 --> 00:44:52,830
Now we have this generalization problem

888
00:44:52,830 --> 00:44:55,370
and we actually also have a way to address this

889
00:44:55,370 --> 00:44:59,850
So instead of using this task identifier directly

890
00:44:59,850 --> 00:45:03,510
we can actually try to learn a more compressed representation

891
00:45:03,510 --> 00:45:07,270
of the task that will generalize more effectively

892
00:45:07,270 --> 00:45:08,990
than a one-hot vector

893
00:45:08,990 --> 00:45:13,430
So as an example, when we learn the execution policy

894
00:45:13,430 --> 00:45:15,390
we're also going to identify information that's

895
00:45:15,390 --> 00:45:17,470
needed for solving the task

896
00:45:17,470 --> 00:45:19,700
And so say that our identifier consists

897
00:45:19,700 --> 00:45:21,070
of a few things that are relevant

898
00:45:21,070 --> 00:45:23,110
and a few things that are not relevant

899
00:45:23,110 --> 00:45:24,870
So it contains things like wall color

900
00:45:24,870 --> 00:45:26,550
and decorations, but then it also

901
00:45:26,550 --> 00:45:30,030
contains information about where the ingredients are

902
00:45:30,030 --> 00:45:32,170
Then we'd like to use-- and similar

903
00:45:32,170 --> 00:45:33,920
if you had a one-hot vector, it would also

904
00:45:33,920 --> 00:45:35,820
contain information that might be irrelevant

905
00:45:35,820 --> 00:45:38,200
because you might have two Kitchens that have ingredients

906
00:45:38,200 --> 00:45:41,280
in the same place, but then have other things that are spurious

907
00:45:41,280 --> 00:45:43,640
And we'll try to then bottleneck this representation

908
00:45:43,640 --> 00:45:45,840
to compress away the stuff that's not

909
00:45:45,840 --> 00:45:50,440
needed for solving the task

910
00:45:50,440 --> 00:45:53,080
And if we can do this, then this sort of representation

911
00:45:53,080 --> 00:45:55,560
should generalize better

912
00:45:55,560 --> 00:45:58,420
And then when we learn to explore

913
00:45:58,420 --> 00:46:00,640
instead of learning to explore to be

914
00:46:00,640 --> 00:46:02,340
able to predict the identifier here

915
00:46:02,340 --> 00:46:03,720
we'll learn to explore so that we

916
00:46:03,720 --> 00:46:08,800
can recover this compressed task representation

917
00:46:08,800 --> 00:46:12,400
And so try to be able to explore so that you're

918
00:46:12,400 --> 00:46:19,880
able to predict what this z representation for the task is

919
00:46:19,880 --> 00:46:23,360
Now, how do you actually compress representations

920
00:46:23,360 --> 00:46:24,300
in neural networks

921
00:46:24,300 --> 00:46:26,420
So before we go into the details of this algorithm

922
00:46:26,420 --> 00:46:29,320
let's talk about that a little bit

923
00:46:29,320 --> 00:46:39,610
So we have our task identifier mu, mu i

924
00:46:39,610 --> 00:46:44,210
and then we'd like to have a neural network that

925
00:46:44,210 --> 00:46:49,770
uses that to predict zi

926
00:46:49,770 --> 00:46:51,650
And then, we'll then have a policy

927
00:46:51,650 --> 00:46:58,770
that takes this and the state and outputs the action

928
00:46:58,770 --> 00:47:04,320
And if we train this whole thing end to end, it may not--

929
00:47:04,320 --> 00:47:06,130
it's not necessarily incentivized

930
00:47:06,130 --> 00:47:08,890
to actually compress information here

931
00:47:08,890 --> 00:47:13,610
to actually remove information about decorations, for example

932
00:47:13,610 --> 00:47:18,490
So one thing we could do is we could simply add noise

933
00:47:18,490 --> 00:47:21,370
to this representation

934
00:47:21,370 --> 00:47:24,810
And so if you take this, and instead

935
00:47:24,810 --> 00:47:30,700
of feeding it directly here, you could take this output zi

936
00:47:30,700 --> 00:47:34,920
And then right here, you could basically add some noise

937
00:47:34,920 --> 00:47:36,940
So right here, what you're doing is

938
00:47:36,940 --> 00:47:41,820
you're adding some noise where the noise is sampled

939
00:47:41,820 --> 00:47:45,140
from a Gaussian distribution

940
00:47:45,140 --> 00:47:49,460
And if you add noise, then this is going to discard information

941
00:47:49,460 --> 00:47:51,300
because you're adding--

942
00:47:51,300 --> 00:47:54,700
you're kind of corrupting the signal

943
00:47:54,700 --> 00:47:58,660
But you might discard some good information

944
00:47:58,660 --> 00:48:01,520
especially if you do this only at test time

945
00:48:01,520 --> 00:48:03,100
Now in principle, you could also train

946
00:48:03,100 --> 00:48:08,700
with this noise to encourage it to discard information from z

947
00:48:08,700 --> 00:48:12,860
But if you train the policy with this noise as input

948
00:48:12,860 --> 00:48:15,860
then what it could just do is it could just

949
00:48:15,860 --> 00:48:18,020
increase the magnitude of zi here

950
00:48:18,020 --> 00:48:19,460
so that the magnitude of the noise

951
00:48:19,460 --> 00:48:22,550
is a lot smaller in comparison

952
00:48:22,550 --> 00:48:24,180
And so if you just add noise, the model

953
00:48:24,180 --> 00:48:27,830
can compensate and still pass a lot of information

954
00:48:27,830 --> 00:48:31,430
through this task representation

955
00:48:31,430 --> 00:48:36,770
So that's not a great solution, but what we can do

956
00:48:36,770 --> 00:48:38,310
is we can just also prevent the model

957
00:48:38,310 --> 00:48:40,470
from increasing the magnitude

958
00:48:40,470 --> 00:48:44,750
And we can just add a loss that--

959
00:48:44,750 --> 00:48:47,590
we can modify our training loss for the policy

960
00:48:47,590 --> 00:48:55,710
to include a term that's going to minimize the magnitude of z

961
00:48:55,710 --> 00:48:58,350
And if you do this, you're then--

962
00:48:58,350 --> 00:49:00,870
now if it kind of blows up the magnitude of z

963
00:49:00,870 --> 00:49:04,930
then it will have a high loss here

964
00:49:04,930 --> 00:49:07,150
And so it will be forced to keep that magnitude down

965
00:49:07,150 --> 00:49:11,430
to be lower, so that it is actually forced to remove

966
00:49:11,430 --> 00:49:14,230
information that isn't needed

967
00:49:14,230 --> 00:49:16,610
And any sort of magnitude it does introduce

968
00:49:16,610 --> 00:49:19,190
it will be incentivized to only have information that's needed

969
00:49:19,190 --> 00:49:22,950
for predicting the action

970
00:49:22,950 --> 00:49:27,000
This is called a variational information bottleneck, which

971
00:49:27,000 --> 00:49:30,120
is a fancy term for saying that you're adding noise

972
00:49:30,120 --> 00:49:32,200
and then also modifying your loss

973
00:49:32,200 --> 00:49:38,180
term to have this L2 penalty on your representation

974
00:49:38,180 --> 00:49:40,800
One of the reasons why this is called a variational information

975
00:49:40,800 --> 00:49:44,480
bottleneck is that this objective is actually

976
00:49:44,480 --> 00:49:51,800
equivalent to minimizing the KL divergence between your--

977
00:49:51,800 --> 00:49:53,760
If you think about this as a distribution

978
00:49:53,760 --> 00:49:55,890
because this is now kind of a Gaussian distribution

979
00:49:55,890 --> 00:49:58,360
with a mean of zi and a variance according

980
00:49:58,360 --> 00:50:01,970
to the variance of epsilon, that's

981
00:50:01,970 --> 00:50:03,720
equivalent to minimizing the KL divergence

982
00:50:03,720 --> 00:50:09,500
between that distribution and the unit Gaussian distribution

983
00:50:09,500 --> 00:50:11,000
Yeah, so the question is, why are we

984
00:50:11,000 --> 00:50:14,820
creating a representation of a one-hot vector

985
00:50:14,820 --> 00:50:17,200
So if we go back to the algorithm

986
00:50:17,200 --> 00:50:19,800
we talked about before, where we are

987
00:50:19,800 --> 00:50:22,300
exploring to generate experience

988
00:50:22,300 --> 00:50:24,500
then trying to identify the task, and then

989
00:50:24,500 --> 00:50:29,140
condition on that task, if we then want to quickly solve

990
00:50:29,140 --> 00:50:34,340
a new task, then we'll be able to use our policy to explore

991
00:50:34,340 --> 00:50:39,060
But then after we explore, if our representation is

992
00:50:39,060 --> 00:50:41,720
a one-hot vector here, if it's a new task

993
00:50:41,720 --> 00:50:43,900
then that new task may not cleanly

994
00:50:43,900 --> 00:50:46,680
fit within one of the previous one-hot vectors

995
00:50:46,680 --> 00:50:49,380
And so we'd like to have a more continuous space that's

996
00:50:49,380 --> 00:50:53,140
more compactly representing the task

997
00:50:53,140 --> 00:50:56,540
and essentially learn this latent task representation that

998
00:50:56,540 --> 00:50:58,920
will generalize to new tasks

999
00:50:58,920 --> 00:51:00,340
Yeah, so the question is, don't we

1000
00:51:00,340 --> 00:51:02,100
need more information about the task

1001
00:51:02,100 --> 00:51:05,460
whether or not two tasks are related to each other

1002
00:51:05,460 --> 00:51:09,500
Though, I guess the--

1003
00:51:09,500 --> 00:51:11,660
so when you think about a one-hot

1004
00:51:11,660 --> 00:51:14,860
it doesn't really seem like it is necessarily capturing that

1005
00:51:14,860 --> 00:51:19,220
much information, but a one-hot vector is actually less

1006
00:51:19,220 --> 00:51:27,030
compressed than something that is--

1007
00:51:27,030 --> 00:51:30,230
something that's a more continuous or less

1008
00:51:30,230 --> 00:51:31,870
discrete representation

1009
00:51:31,870 --> 00:51:35,730
So say, for example, let's see

1010
00:51:35,730 --> 00:51:39,250
Let's say, for example, that one of your tasks is to go here

1011
00:51:39,250 --> 00:51:41,390
This is task one

1012
00:51:41,390 --> 00:51:43,870
Task two is to go here

1013
00:51:43,870 --> 00:51:49,110
Task three is to go here, and task four is to go here

1014
00:51:49,110 --> 00:51:51,790
Then task one and task four are very similar

1015
00:51:51,790 --> 00:51:56,830
but when you use one-hot vectors to represent them

1016
00:51:56,830 --> 00:52:02,470
they are kind of represented completely separately

1017
00:52:02,470 --> 00:52:08,030
And if you try to learn a task representation that's

1018
00:52:08,030 --> 00:52:09,790
compressing these, then it will actually

1019
00:52:09,790 --> 00:52:14,790
learn a representation that puts these much closer to each other

1020
00:52:14,790 --> 00:52:17,310
The question is, do you need to add epsilon to the loss

1021
00:52:17,310 --> 00:52:22,960
So it doesn't end up coming into the loss

1022
00:52:22,960 --> 00:52:25,160
but you do need to add it when you're actually

1023
00:52:25,160 --> 00:52:26,960
training the neural network

1024
00:52:26,960 --> 00:52:30,040
So you're adding the noise and you sample the noise

1025
00:52:30,040 --> 00:52:33,800
every-- resample the noise every single time

1026
00:52:33,800 --> 00:52:36,740
And the epsilon is sampled from this distribution

1027
00:52:36,740 --> 00:52:38,560
and you can think about this distribution

1028
00:52:38,560 --> 00:52:43,440
as coming into the loss

1029
00:52:43,440 --> 00:52:47,360
But yeah, you don't need to add epsilon anywhere into the loss

1030
00:52:47,360 --> 00:52:49,960
It just comes out to this

1031
00:52:49,960 --> 00:52:54,200
So let's walk through, actually, what this algorithm looks

1032
00:52:54,200 --> 00:52:57,880
like more completely

1033
00:52:57,880 --> 00:53:04,440
So first, let's talk about just executing on the task

1034
00:53:04,440 --> 00:53:06,100
separate from exploration

1035
00:53:06,100 --> 00:53:08,640
So our goal is to learn this task representation, this latent

1036
00:53:08,640 --> 00:53:12,320
task representation, and how to solve the task

1037
00:53:12,320 --> 00:53:14,640
And to do this, we're going to have--

1038
00:53:14,640 --> 00:53:16,800
we have our task identifier

1039
00:53:16,800 --> 00:53:24,130
And we're going to have a network that produces zi

1040
00:53:24,130 --> 00:53:32,210
We're going to add noise to get zi plus epsilon

1041
00:53:32,210 --> 00:53:41,120
And then we're going to pass this and the state into our--

1042
00:53:41,120 --> 00:53:44,090
this is our execution policy that's

1043
00:53:44,090 --> 00:53:48,450
going to produce an action

1044
00:53:48,450 --> 00:53:51,420
And we can think about this as having two parts

1045
00:53:51,420 --> 00:53:53,170
so we have the execution policy right here

1046
00:53:53,170 --> 00:53:57,530
that takes as input the noisy zi and the state

1047
00:53:57,530 --> 00:53:58,910
to produce the action

1048
00:53:58,910 --> 00:54:01,770
And then we also have this part right here

1049
00:54:01,770 --> 00:54:08,570
And we'll write this as some encoder F that maps from mu

1050
00:54:08,570 --> 00:54:13,090
i to zi

1051
00:54:13,090 --> 00:54:15,690
We can write this as a distribution

1052
00:54:15,690 --> 00:54:22,060
because if you fold this noise into a distribution like this

1053
00:54:22,060 --> 00:54:27,660
this will be some distribution that is--

1054
00:54:27,660 --> 00:54:29,940
let's now call this z bar

1055
00:54:29,940 --> 00:54:35,060
This is a distribution that is a Gaussian with mean

1056
00:54:35,060 --> 00:54:43,780
of z bar i and some variance

1057
00:54:43,780 --> 00:54:47,260
Let's just write it as variance, like that

1058
00:54:47,260 --> 00:54:51,660
So these are the two things that have parameters, F and pi

1059
00:54:51,660 --> 00:54:54,660
And we're going to train both of these neural networks

1060
00:54:54,660 --> 00:54:58,100
to maximize reward, so that we're actually

1061
00:54:58,100 --> 00:55:01,180
learning a representation, such that we can solve the task

1062
00:55:01,180 --> 00:55:03,780
and predict actions that maximize reward

1063
00:55:03,780 --> 00:55:08,980
And then we're also going to add the regularization on z

1064
00:55:08,980 --> 00:55:12,340
And so our objective here will be

1065
00:55:12,340 --> 00:55:17,510
maximizing the sum of expected rewards

1066
00:55:17,510 --> 00:55:25,270
under our execution policy

1067
00:55:25,270 --> 00:55:27,630
And since we're maximizing here, we're

1068
00:55:27,630 --> 00:55:31,910
also going to be minimizing this term right here, which

1069
00:55:31,910 --> 00:55:34,130
is equivalent to the KL divergence

1070
00:55:34,130 --> 00:55:37,390
So we'll be maximizing the negative KL

1071
00:55:37,390 --> 00:55:48,790
divergence between F of zi, given mu i

1072
00:55:48,790 --> 00:55:55,070
and this unit Gaussian

1073
00:55:55,070 --> 00:55:58,370
So on the slides, there's the objective

1074
00:55:58,370 --> 00:56:00,330
And this is just trying to solve the task

1075
00:56:00,330 --> 00:56:04,230
and also learn a latent task representation based off

1076
