01:09:49,160 --> 01:09:50,270
Cool

1335
01:09:50,270 --> 01:09:52,640
We have a little bit of time, and so you could actually--

1336
01:09:52,640 --> 01:09:54,660
I wanted to briefly talk about one application

1337
01:09:54,660 --> 01:09:56,800
So you can take this algorithm and use it

1338
01:09:56,800 --> 01:09:58,920
in a domain that's a little bit different from what

1339
01:09:58,920 --> 01:10:00,640
we've seen before

1340
01:10:00,640 --> 01:10:04,960
And this domain is trying to find bugs in different computer

1341
01:10:04,960 --> 01:10:10,110
programs, and in particular, in intro computer science courses

1342
01:10:10,110 --> 01:10:12,320
although this would probably apply to other programs

1343
01:10:12,320 --> 01:10:14,200
as well

1344
01:10:14,200 --> 01:10:15,880
It's really cool for students to be

1345
01:10:15,880 --> 01:10:19,760
able to make computer programs

1346
01:10:19,760 --> 01:10:22,000
It's very rewarding for them to write a program

1347
01:10:22,000 --> 01:10:23,640
as they're learning how to code

1348
01:10:23,640 --> 01:10:26,460
Then that creates a program like this

1349
01:10:26,460 --> 01:10:30,120
So on code.org, students code up this game

1350
01:10:30,120 --> 01:10:34,320
called Bounce that looks a bit like Pong, or one side of Pong

1351
01:10:34,320 --> 01:10:37,880
where the goal is to hit a ball into the goal

1352
01:10:37,880 --> 01:10:41,720
In CS106A at Stanford, students code up Breakout

1353
01:10:41,720 --> 01:10:44,420
and so they might code up a program like this

1354
01:10:44,420 --> 01:10:49,410
This is an example of a buggy game, where the ball actually

1355
01:10:49,410 --> 01:10:51,550
kind of gets skewered by the paddle

1356
01:10:51,550 --> 01:10:52,970
It kind of bounces off the paddle

1357
01:10:52,970 --> 01:10:57,090
rather than going and losing a point or losing a life

1358
01:10:57,090 --> 01:11:00,290
And we can basically treat different student programs

1359
01:11:00,290 --> 01:11:04,170
as different tasks, where our goal is to explore and find

1360
01:11:04,170 --> 01:11:06,170
the bugs that might exist

1361
01:11:06,170 --> 01:11:08,090
And our ultimate goal is to then be

1362
01:11:08,090 --> 01:11:10,770
able to provide feedback to the student on the different bugs

1363
01:11:10,770 --> 01:11:12,670
in their program

1364
01:11:12,670 --> 01:11:14,090
One of the other things about this

1365
01:11:14,090 --> 01:11:15,730
is it's really time consuming for TAs

1366
01:11:15,730 --> 01:11:17,810
or instructors to actually grade and give feedback

1367
01:11:17,810 --> 01:11:19,440
on these assignments, because you often

1368
01:11:19,440 --> 01:11:23,110
end up playing the game in order to figure out and find the bugs

1369
01:11:23,110 --> 01:11:23,610
Cool

1370
01:11:23,610 --> 01:11:24,850
So we can use meta-RL

1371
01:11:24,850 --> 01:11:27,250
We can actually use exactly the DREAM algorithm

1372
01:11:27,250 --> 01:11:30,450
and train it to be able to identify

1373
01:11:30,450 --> 01:11:35,450
different distinguishing factors from different students

1374
01:11:35,450 --> 01:11:37,130
And it can learn exploration strategies

1375
01:11:37,130 --> 01:11:39,470
like what happens if the ball hits the goal

1376
01:11:39,470 --> 01:11:41,970
So here, it's trying to actually play the game successfully

1377
01:11:41,970 --> 01:11:44,880
It can also check and act, so what happens

1378
01:11:44,880 --> 01:11:46,000
if the ball hits the floor

1379
01:11:46,000 --> 01:11:49,100
So it's actually purposely trying to not hit the ball

1380
01:11:49,100 --> 01:11:51,780
or also try to hit the wall instead of the goal

1381
01:11:51,780 --> 01:11:53,740
And this is an example of a buggy program that

1382
01:11:53,740 --> 01:11:57,060
creates lots of different balls as soon as the ball hits

1383
01:11:57,060 --> 01:12:00,140
the wall

1384
01:12:00,140 --> 01:12:04,020
And then there was an experiment in 106A two years ago

1385
01:12:04,020 --> 01:12:08,060
in the spring 2020 quarter, where TAs actually used

1386
01:12:08,060 --> 01:12:11,820
the meta-RL algorithm in the policy to basically grade

1387
01:12:11,820 --> 01:12:14,780
and pre-populate the rubric for grading

1388
01:12:14,780 --> 01:12:17,540
And then they just verified that the rubric answers

1389
01:12:17,540 --> 01:12:20,820
were correct from watching the videos of the meta-RL agent

1390
01:12:20,820 --> 01:12:22,580
playing the game

1391
01:12:22,580 --> 01:12:26,660
And it led to 44% faster grading, and also 6% more

1392
01:12:26,660 --> 01:12:30,460
accurate grading, and the TAs generally liked using it

1393
01:12:30,460 --> 01:12:32,300
They also used it in at least one

1394
01:12:32,300 --> 01:12:37,530
subsequent quarter because they liked using it so much

